title: Automatic retention
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
aliases: retention log-retention-revisions
summary: The log keeps the last 200 revisions behind HEAD by default, labelled ones never trimmed; the trim rides the write that triggers it.

A watched repository writes a revision per batch of filesystem events and would keep
them for ever; most of that history is of no interest. Retention is pruning as a standing
policy. Two settings, per repository (its `config.json`) or daemon-wide (`[settings]`,
[[Daemon configuration]]):

* ''`log-retention-revisions`'' — how many revisions to keep behind HEAD; default 200, `0` keeps everything. A revision is an ''event'' — a batch of filesystem changes, one command — not an operation, and one event routinely carries thousands of operations; 200 is far more history than anyone walks back through.
* ''`log-retention-keep-labels`'' — when true (the default), a labelled revision is a floor: nothing older than the oldest label goes, and the log grows past the limit rather than lose a named checkpoint. Label a revision (`PATCH /log/revisions/:id`, or with the write) to say "keep everything from here".

The limit being on by default, a repository is trimmed on its first write after the
setting takes effect — permanently, without asking. That is the point (an unbounded log is
what it protects against), but a one-way door: a repository whose whole history matters
wants `log-retention-revisions = 0`, or a label, ''before'' it is written again.

!! What a trim does

A trim is a `prune before` whose target is the first operation of the oldest revision to
keep — same rules, same ''weak root'' afterwards ([[Pruning the log]]). It runs ''inside
the transaction of the write that triggered it'', after HEAD moved: a range deletion of the
oldest operations and their snapshots, cheaper than the append that triggered it, riding a
commit that was being paid for anyway. It does not run at every write: the log may drift a
tenth of the limit past it (at least one revision) first — so the oldest, coldest pages are
dirtied once per slack, not at every commit.

!! When a trim declines

The cut must be an ancestor of HEAD, as for an explicit prune. After a rollback it may not
be — the newest revisions by id sit on the abandoned branch, and cutting there would delete
the history HEAD stands on. The trim then does nothing and the log grows past the limit,
temporarily: as the current line becomes the newest again, the cut lands on it, and the
abandoned branch goes with everything below it.

A trim happens only on a write: a repository nobody writes to keeps what it has. The
repository's creation (its root metarecord) is never trimmed — there is nothing before it.
